course: dictionary
lesson: 6578
title: Nil: nothing, zero, and nil by mouth
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Nil means nothing, or zero. You will meet it in sport, in formal writing and in hospitals. Read the words around it to find its meaning, just as you would with any new word.
explain: In sport, nil is the score for zero. «We won three–nil» means we scored three goals and the other team scored none. Two–nil is another score, and nil–nil means neither team scored at all.
explain: Nil also appears in formal language. «The risk is nil» means there is no risk at all. A hospital sign that says nil by mouth is not a score: the words tell you it is a rule about food and drink.
fact: Nil means nothing or zero.
fact: In UK, Irish, Australian and New Zealand sport talk, nil is used for zero in match scores, as in «two–nil».
fact: «Nil–nil» means neither team scored.
fact: «Nil by mouth» means no food or drink, often before an operation.
fact: The word líng also means zero, as in «líng bǐ sān» (nil–three) and «yìbǎi líng wǔ» (105).
exercise: «We won three–nil.» What does nil mean in this score? | The game was called off | Half-time | *Zero, so the other team scored no goals | Three goals each
exercise: «The risk is nil.» What does nil mean here? | The risk is paid for | *There is no risk at all | There is a little risk | There is a very big risk
exercise: Which phrase tells a patient to have no food or drink? | *Nil by mouth | Two–nil | Nil–nil
test: «The match ended nil–nil.» How many goals did each team score? | One each | *None | Two each | Nine
test: In «líng bǐ sān» (nil–three), what does líng mean? | *Zero | Nine | Stop | Three
exam: A hospital sign says nil by mouth before an operation. What should the patient do? | Ask for a sandwich | Drink lots of water | Eat a big meal | *Have no food or drink
